        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "efeba6a7-d465-519e-9b40-49dd3153d02f",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Hart Crane, John le Carré, T. S. Eliot\n- Works: The Bridge, The Broken Tower, The Waste Land, The Russia House\n- Roles: British author, espionage novelist\n- Organizations: Security Service, Secret Intelligence Service\n\nFacts:\n- Hart Crane sought in his most ambitious work, \"The Bridge,\" to write an epic poem in the vein of T. S. Eliot's \"The Waste Land\" while expressing a more optimistic view of modern urban culture than he found in Eliot's work.\n- David John Moore Cornwell was born on 19 October 1931 and became known by the pseudonym John le Carré.\n- During the 1950s and the 1960s, John le Carré worked for both the Security Service and the Secret Intelligence Service and began writing novels under his pen name.\n- \"The Broken Tower\" was published in 1932 as the last new poem meant to be published in Hart Crane's lifetime and has been widely regarded as one of the best lyrics of his last years or his career.\n- John le Carré was a British author of espionage novels.\n- \"The Russia House\" is a spy novel by John le Carré that was published in 1989.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "a7784833-7bd2-5d64-815e-0984dd230d7a",
        "text": "Page: Hart Crane. Sentence: In his most ambitious work, \"The Bridge\", Crane sought to write an epic poem, in the vein of \"The Waste Land\", that expressed a more optimistic view of modern, urban culture than the one that he found in Eliot's work.\nPage: John le Carré. Sentence: David John Moore Cornwell (born 19 October 1931), known by the pseudonym John le Carré ( ), is a British author of espionage novels.\nPage: John le Carré. Sentence: During the 1950s and the 1960s, he worked for both the Security Service and the Secret Intelligence Service, and began writing novels under his pen name.\nPage: The Broken Tower. Sentence: The last new poem meant to be published in Hart Crane's life, \"The Broken Tower\" (1932) has been widely acknowledged as one of the best lyrics of Crane's last years, if not his career.\nPage: The Russia House. Sentence: The Russia House is a spy novel by John le Carré published in 1989.",
